{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GB2WqfruenUmP4evWFKg7j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TBM5QePYwDRbaPHUxT3JZV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T214352/best/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4355,"cache_read_input_tokens":62055,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4355,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N7EgbKWRQihTMUsrWaVab2","session_id":"afaa0581-5224-4e41-930b-491fc2855128","uuid":"8aafa850-bdf4-4b18-aed4-8f6d611fbaef"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TBM5QePYwDRbaPHUxT3JZV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\renewcommand{\\bibsection}{\\section*{References}}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\fone}{F\\textsubscript{1}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\fstagone}{F\\textsubscript{1,stag}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Stagnation Signals Are Structural: An Empirical Study of\\\\Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLLM-based agents frequently enter stagnation during long-horizon tasks---executing plausible but collectively ineffective action sequences.\n28\tWe present a systematic empirical study of progress stagnation detection through weak supervision over agent trajectories.\n29\tOur central finding is that stagnation signals are fundamentally structural: 30 lightweight features (command repetition ratio, file diff frequency, action entropy) achieve 0.995 in-domain and 0.967 cross-agent macro-\\fone{}, decisively outperforming neural embedding probes (0.906/0.751) and an LLM-as-judge baseline by over 23 percentage points.\n30\tHuman annotation of 650 trajectory windows confirms this hierarchy is not a circular evaluation artifact: on gold labels, structural features attain 0.91 \\fone{} vs.\\ 0.85 for the best probe and 0.68 for LLM-as-judge, with an 8.5pp circularity gap that inflates all learned methods equally.\n31\tWe further discover that labeling functions exhibit systematic temporal dynamics---command repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps while outcome backpropagation lags by +6.7 steps---but these roles are agent-specific, with command repetition reversing from leading to lagging on a different agent framework.\n32\tDespite these well-characterized temporal patterns motivating a temporal label model, three implementations (time-weighted voting, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to surpass simple majority vote, with time-weighted labels degrading downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp.\n33\tOur results redirect agent monitoring from expensive representation learning toward lightweight structural approaches.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t%======================================================================\n37\t\\section{Introduction}\n38\t\\label{sec:intro}\n39\t%======================================================================\n40\t\n41\tLLM-based coding agents such as SWE-Agent \\cite{yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands \\cite{wang2024openhands} can autonomously solve software engineering tasks, yet they frequently enter prolonged periods of \\emph{stagnation}: the agent executes commands that appear diverse and locally reasonable but collectively fail to make progress toward the goal.\n42\tDetecting such stagnation in real time is essential for efficient resource allocation, early intervention, and runtime monitoring of autonomous agents.\n43\t\n44\tPrior work has approached agent failure detection through cycle detectors that identify literal action repetition \\cite{jiang2024cdcs}, process reward models (PRMs) that score individual steps \\cite{xi2026agentprm}, and LLM self-reflection mechanisms \\cite{shinn2023reflexion}.\n45\tThese methods either miss semantically diverse stagnation (cycle detectors), require expensive outcome labels and RL pipelines (PRMs), or rely on the judgment of the very model that is stuck (self-reflection).\n46\tRecent evidence from SlopCodeBench \\cite{slopcodebench2026} shows that agent degradation---including verbosity inflation in 90\\% of trajectories---is pervasive, motivating systematic monitoring approaches.\n47\t\n48\tWe study stagnation detection as a window-level binary classification problem: given a window of $k{=}5$ consecutive agent actions, predict whether the agent is making progress or stagnating.\n49\tWe construct labels via a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions (LFs)---command repetition, file diff absence, and outcome backpropagation---aggregated by majority vote.\n50\tUsing this framework, we conduct a systematic comparison of structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge baselines across two agent frameworks (SWE-Agent and OpenHands) on 12{,}000+ trajectory windows derived from SWE-bench Verified instances."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N7EgbKWRQihTMUsrWaVab2","session_id":"afaa0581-5224-4e41-930b-491fc2855128","uuid":"1fcc527b-7a1e-48e3-9743-7ab3af68723b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:30:54.051Z"}
