{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GdobHcVcqGQon44cqpBPfF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of both the submitted paper (docs/paper/) and the \"dream\" version (which simulates what the paper would look like with gold set results). Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper studies progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents, comparing structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge approaches under a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions. The central claim is that stagnation signals are \"fundamentally structural.\" The actual submitted paper (docs/paper/) contains a critical gap: the gold set validation section (Section 5.7) is a placeholder stating \"pending,\" meaning the paper's primary non-circular evaluation does not exist at submission time. A \"dream\" version simulates what gold results might look like, but the submitted manuscript relies almost entirely on silver-label evaluation, which the authors themselves acknowledge is circular for their headline result (structural RF 0.9951).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Honest circular evaluation framing.** The paper is unusually transparent about the circularity problem: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels, so 0.9951 is an LF-reconstructibility score, not a detection score. This intellectual honesty is commendable and rare.\n- **Well-designed cmd_rep ablation on OpenHands.** The finding that removing cmd_rep improves OH probe F1 by 17.6pp (Table 5 in the submitted version) is a genuinely useful insight for the weak supervision community, demonstrating that LFs validated on one agent can become adversarial on another.\n- **Temporal dynamics analysis is rigorous.** The Kruskal-Wallis test with Bonferroni-corrected pairwise comparisons (H=274.3, p=2.74e-60) properly establishes that LF temporal roles are statistically distinct.\n- **Negative result on temporal label models is well-motivated and well-analyzed.** The paper traces the failure to the low-conflict regime (95.1% conf>=2 agreement) and provides a clear mechanistic explanation (TWV flips labels in the wrong direction). This constrains future research meaningfully.\n- **Comprehensive ablation suite.** Encoder ablation (3 backbones), window size ablation (k=3,5,7), data scaling, and multi-seed stability are all present.\n- **Practical relevance.** The <5ms inference time and no-GPU requirement for structural features have clear deployment value.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **[Major] Gold set results are missing from the submitted paper.** Section 5.7 is a blue-text placeholder: \"Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending. Results will be added upon completion (expected 2026-05-17).\" The conclusion states the gold set \"will determine whether structural features detect real stagnation or merely reconstruct labeling function outputs.\" Without this section, the paper cannot support its title claim. The authors explicitly frame this as the GO/NO-GO gate in their ROADMAP. Submitting with this placeholder is below the threshold for review.\n\n- **[Major] Circular evaluation undermines the headline claim.** The paper's title and abstract assert stagnation signals are \"fundamentally structural,\" but the primary evidence (0.9951 in-domain F1) is acknowledged as circular. Cross-agent transfer (0.967) is offered as non-circular, but this too evaluates against silver labels on the target agent---labels that also suffer from the same LF-feature overlap. The only truly non-circular evaluation is the missing gold set.\n\n- **[Major] The \"circular evaluation\" concept is never formalized.** The paper describes circularity informally (\"structural features overlap with the signals used by labeling functions\") but never defines it precisely. What exactly constitutes \"overlap\"? Is it feature-LF mutual information? Functional dependency? The absence of a formal definition makes the inflation quantification (Table 4 in the task description / not present in the submitted paper) ad hoc. A formal treatment (e.g., defining circularity as the mutual information between feature vector X and LF vote vector Lambda, or showing that structural features can exactly reconstruct LF votes) would strengthen the contribution.\n\n- **[Major] No confidence intervals on main results.** Table 2 (main results) reports point estimates for all methods. Multi-seed stability is mentioned only for structural RF (0.9951 +/- 0.0000) and probe (0.9185 +/- 0.0113) in the text but not in the table. Significance tests between methods are not reported. The paired bootstrap test mentioned in the ROADMAP appears to still be in progress (\"paired-bootstrap cross-domain fix Worker running\"). Without these, the claimed gaps (e.g., structural-probe gap of 8.96pp) cannot be assessed for statistical reliability.\n\n- **[Major] No figures in the submitted paper except pipeline, temporal, and data scaling.** The paper has exactly three figures (pipeline diagram, LF temporal dynamics, data scaling curve). Conspicuously absent: confusion matrices, error analysis visualizations, Sankey diagrams for LF agreement, feature importance bar plots, cross-agent comparison visualizations. For an empirical analysis paper, this visual poverty is a significant weakness. The encoder ablation (Appendix Table 7) has incomplete per-seed detail (\"per-seed detail not recorded\").\n\n- **[Minor] Gold set construction details are insufficient for the submitted version.** The submitted paper mentions a \"650-window gold set with a validated annotation pipeline\" in the limitations section but provides no details about annotator selection, annotation guidelines, adjudication protocol, or sampling strategy. The dream version adds IAA kappa=0.82 and agreement=91%, but these are simulated, not actual.\n\n- **[Minor] Binary stagnation formulation is coarse.** The paper acknowledges this in limitations but does not explore even simple extensions (e.g., a confidence-weighted prediction, a 3-class scheme with an \"ambiguous\" category). Given that conf=1 windows have only 69% gold accuracy (per the dream version), this is a real limitation.\n\n- **[Minor] LLM-as-judge baseline is weak.** Zero-shot Claude Sonnet with no chain-of-thought, no few-shot examples, and a single prompt variant is a strawman. The paper does not explore whether providing the LLM with structural feature values, few-shot examples of stagnation, or explicit rubrics would improve performance. This matters because the 33.3pp gap over LLM-as-judge is a headline number.\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Gold set evaluation (the entire Section 5.7).** This is the single most important missing piece. Without it, the paper cannot distinguish genuine detection from LF reconstruction.\n\n2. **Significance testing between methods.** Paired bootstrap or McNemar's test between structural vs. probe, structural vs. LLM, and probe vs. LLM on the same test set.\n\n3. **Feature importance shift analysis.** The paper_framing mentions cmd_rep_ratio rank shifting from 1->3 on gold vs. LF-derived evaluation, but this analysis is absent from the submitted paper. The appendix says \"Feature importance ranking from RF/GB models is pending a dedicated analysis run.\"\n\n4. **Cross-agent gold evaluation.** Even a small gold set on OpenHands (e.g., 100 windows) would validate the cross-agent transfer claim non-circularly. The dream version acknowledges this is missing and uses \"bounded estimates.\"\n\n5. **LLM-as-judge with chain-of-thought or few-shot.** The current zero-shot baseline is minimal.\n\n6. **Error overlap Venn diagram across all three methods** (structural, probe, LLM). The error analysis in Section 6.3 covers only the probe; the dream version adds a comparison but the submitted paper lacks it.\n\n7. **Calibration analysis.** Are the models' predicted probabilities well-calibrated? This matters for downstream use in intervention systems.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Will the gold set be completed before submission?** The ROADMAP indicates May 16 as the latest start date and May 25 as the deadline. If the gold set is not included, what claim structure survives? The paper currently hedges with \"will determine\" language, which is not acceptable for a submitted paper.\n\n2. **How do you define \"circular evaluation\" formally?** You state structural features \"overlap with\" LF inputs, but 30 features include many that are NOT direct LF inputs (text length statistics, error signals, command type distributions). Can you quantify what fraction of the structural feature variance is explained by the 3 LF votes? A simple experiment: train a model to predict the 3 LF votes from the 30 features and measure R-squared.\n\n3. **Why does the cross-agent transfer constitute non-circular evaluation?** The OpenHands labels are also silver (produced by the same 3 LFs). The LF firing patterns differ, but the labels are still LF-derived. Is the argument that because the LF distributions differ, the classifier cannot trivially reconstruct them? This needs more precision.\n\n4. **What is the stagnation prevalence discrepancy between LF (28.5%) and human annotation?** The dream version reports 33% stagnation in the gold set vs. 28.5% in silver labels. This 4.5pp gap suggests LFs systematically under-label stagnation. Does this affect the inflation quantification?\n\n5. **Why not include a supervised baseline trained on a small gold set?** With 650 gold-annotated windows, a train/test split (e.g., 500/150) could provide a non-circular supervised upper bound.\n\n### Detailed Comments\n\nThe paper occupies an uncomfortable position: it identifies a real and important problem (circular evaluation in weak supervision for agent data), frames it honestly, but then fails to resolve it within the submitted manuscript. The gold set section---which the authors themselves identify as the \"GO/NO-GO gate\"---is a placeholder. This creates a situation where the paper's most important claim (\"stagnation signals are fundamentally structural\") rests entirely on evidence the authors themselves flag as potentially circular.\n\nThe dream/simulated version of the paper is substantially stronger, showing a 0.91 gold F1 for structural GB, a clear hierarchy preservation, and an 8.5pp circularity quantification. If these results materialize, the paper would be in the weak accept range. But the submitted version does not contain them.\n\nThe experimental methodology is otherwise sound. The weak supervision pipeline is well-designed, the three LFs target complementary signals, and the systematic comparison across structural/probe/LLM is thorough. The temporal dynamics finding is genuinely novel and the negative result on temporal label models is well-analyzed. The cmd_rep ablation on OpenHands is one of the strongest experimental contributions.\n\nHowever, several design choices weaken the conclusions. The LLM-as-judge baseline is minimal (zero-shot, single prompt, no CoT), making the 33.3pp gap less impressive than it appears. The binary classification formulation discards useful gradient information. The cross-agent evaluation uses only 2 agents from the same domain (software engineering on SWE-bench), limiting generalization claims.\n\nThe paper's framing as an \"empirical analysis paper\" is appropriate given the pivot from temporal label models (which failed) to structural signal discovery. But empirical analysis papers live or die on the quality and completeness of their empirical evidence. A paper that acknowledges its primary evaluation is circular and then does not include the non-circular evaluation is, by its own standard, incomplete.\n\nRegarding reproducibility: the submitted paper provides window size k=5, stride 1, GRU hyperparameters (hidden=256, dropout=0.1, lr=1e-3, batch=32, patience=5), and the full 30-feature list in the appendix. The LLM-as-judge prompt is not provided. Train/test split details are incomplete (the split strategy---random, by instance, by trajectory---is not specified). The labeling function thresholds for cmd_rep and file_diff are not given.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Preliminary Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe score of 3 (Weak Reject) reflects the fundamental incompleteness of the empirical evaluation. The gold set---which the paper itself positions as essential---is missing. If the gold results from the dream simulation materialize and match the simulated numbers, the paper would merit reconsideration at 4/6. The confidence is 4/5 because the missing gold evaluation is a clear, verifiable gap, and the circularity concern is well-articulated by the authors themselves. The remaining uncertainty is whether the gold results, once available, will validate or overturn the claimed hierarchy."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a67b5442dda3709cd (use SendMessage with to: 'a67b5442dda3709cd' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 70255\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 130937</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa0581-5224-4e41-930b-491fc2855128","uuid":"3c47adf2-5889-48b8-8de6-d97f3e61cdc9","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:31:53.352Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Sequential Agent Data\"\n8 pages, anonymous, dated May 14 2026.\n\nThe paper studies progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using weak supervision (labeling functions). The core finding: standard LF-derived evaluation massively overstates detector performance due to \"circular evaluation\"—training and evaluating on labels derived from features that overlap with classifier inputs. They construct a 650-window human-annotated gold set and show:\n\n1. **Inflation**: Structural classifiers drop from F1=0.995 to 0.74-0.76 on gold (25.5pp inflation); probes drop 15.5pp; LLM-as-judge *improves* by 6.8pp on gold.\n2. **Hierarchy collapse**: Structural-over-probe gap reverses from +9pp to -1pp; structural-over-LLM collapses from +24.3pp to +3pp. All methods converge to 0.73-0.77 band.\n3. **Temporal dynamics validated**: LF temporal roles (cmd_rep leading, outcome_bp lagging) preserved on gold (KW p=0.003).\n4. **Temporal label models fail**: TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM all fail to beat majority vote.\n\nContributions claimed: (1) first quantification of circular eval inflation in weak supervision for sequential agent data (mean 21.5pp); (2) 650-window gold benchmark with IAA κ=0.72; (3) temporal dynamics are genuine but agent-specific; (4) negative result on temporal label models.\n\n## Key Tables (from paper)\n- Table 1: Gold set stats: N=650, agreement=86%, κ=0.72, stagnation prevalence=42% (vs LF 28%)\n- Table 2: LF-Human confusion matrix: agreement=68%, κ=0.34, asymmetry ratio 1.93\n- Table 3: Per-LF agreement: cmd_rep 61%, file_diff 78%, outcome_bp 63%\n- Table 4: Inflation table: Structural RF 0.995→0.74 (-25.5pp), Probe 0.906→0.75 (-15.5pp), LLM 0.663→0.73 (+6.8pp), mean -21.5pp\n- Table 5: Gap collapse: Structural-Probe +9→-1pp (reversed), Structural-LLM +24.3→+3pp (-88%)\n- Table 6: Structural on gold: LR 0.71, RF 0.74, GB 0.76 (per-class: GB stag 0.71, prog 0.81)\n- Table 7: Probes on gold: Qwen3 0.75, E5 0.76, BGE 0.77\n- Table 8: Cross-agent (OpenHands): RF 0.68, GB 0.72, Probe-FT 0.70, inflation 23-25pp\n- Table 9: F1_stag breakdown: overall 0.86→0.70, LF-disagrees-with-gold subset 0.41\n- Table 10: Error analysis: RF 169 errors, Probe 163, LLM 176, all-3 overlap 89 (14%)\n- Table 11: Temporal dynamics: cmd_rep -1.87→-0.8, file_diff -1.2→-1.5, outcome_bp +6.7→+4.2, p=0.003\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (imagined experiments list)\nThe full experiment plan included these experiments:\n1. Gold Set — LF-Human Agreement (650 windows, confusion matrix, per-LF agreement)\n2. Gold Set — Structural RF/GB Evaluation (30-dim features on gold, inflation quantification, feature importance shift)\n3. Gold Set — Probe Evaluation (GRU probe on gold, structural-probe gap reversal)\n4. Gold Set — LLM-as-Judge Re-evaluation (Claude Sonnet on gold, deflation analysis)\n5. Gold Set — Cross-Agent Structural on Gold (OH gold subset)\n6. Gold Set — Circular Eval Inflation Quantification (comprehensive inflation/deflation summary)\n7. Gold Set — Inter-Annotator Agreement & Annotation Quality (IAA, adjudication rate)\n8. Gold Set — Error Pattern Re-analysis (error overlap, unique errors, Venn diagram)\n9. F1-Stag Per-Class Breakdown (per-class analysis on LF vs gold, by trajectory length, position, lf_agreement)\n10. Gold Set — Temporal Dynamics Validation (lag analysis on gold)\n11. Gold Set — Paper Reframe Impact Analysis (meta: claim restructuring)\n\nThe paper appears to cover experiments 1-10 substantively. Experiment 11 is meta-analysis.\n\nNotable from the experiment plan:\n- Feature importance shift analysis (cmd_rep_ratio rank 1→3, file_diff_freq rank 3→1) is mentioned in text but no dedicated table\n- Figures described in the plan (Sankey diagram, heatmaps, scatter plots, Venn diagram, dumbbell plot) are NOT present in the paper — the paper has NO figures at all\n- The plan mentions Structural LR baseline; paper includes it in Table 4 and Table 6\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is the experimental design rigorous? Are there confounds?\n- Is the gold set construction (650 windows, 2 annotators, adjudication) adequate?\n- Are the statistical claims supported? (e.g., KW p=0.003, inflation quantification)\n- Is the \"circular evaluation\" mechanism properly formalized or just described informally?\n- Are the baselines appropriate and fairly evaluated?\n\n### 2. Experimental Completeness\n- Which planned experiments are covered vs missing?\n- Are results presented with sufficient detail (confidence intervals, significance tests)?\n- Is the cross-agent evaluation sufficient for generalization claims?\n- Are ablations adequate?\n\n### 3. Reproducibility\n- Are details sufficient to reproduce? (window size k, train/test split, hyperparameters, prompts for LLM-as-judge)\n- Is the gold set described well enough?\n\n### 4. Weaknesses in Methods/Experiments\n- What are the most serious methodological gaps?\n- What experiments would strengthen the paper?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with severity: Major/Minor]\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n[What's absent that should be present]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Paragraph-form deeper analysis]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Preliminary Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a67b5442dda3709cd","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of both the submitted paper (docs/paper/) and the \"dream\" version (which simulates what the paper would look like with gold set results). Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper studies progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents, comparing structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge approaches under a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions. The central claim is that stagnation signals are \"fundamentally structural.\" The actual submitted paper (docs/paper/) contains a critical gap: the gold set validation section (Section 5.7) is a placeholder stating \"pending,\" meaning the paper's primary non-circular evaluation does not exist at submission time. A \"dream\" version simulates what gold results might look like, but the submitted manuscript relies almost entirely on silver-label evaluation, which the authors themselves acknowledge is circular for their headline result (structural RF 0.9951).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Honest circular evaluation framing.** The paper is unusually transparent about the circularity problem: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels, so 0.9951 is an LF-reconstructibility score, not a detection score. This intellectual honesty is commendable and rare.\n- **Well-designed cmd_rep ablation on OpenHands.** The finding that removing cmd_rep improves OH probe F1 by 17.6pp (Table 5 in the submitted version) is a genuinely useful insight for the weak supervision community, demonstrating that LFs validated on one agent can become adversarial on another.\n- **Temporal dynamics analysis is rigorous.** The Kruskal-Wallis test with Bonferroni-corrected pairwise comparisons (H=274.3, p=2.74e-60) properly establishes that LF temporal roles are statistically distinct.\n- **Negative result on temporal label models is well-motivated and well-analyzed.** The paper traces the failure to the low-conflict regime (95.1% conf>=2 agreement) and provides a clear mechanistic explanation (TWV flips labels in the wrong direction). This constrains future research meaningfully.\n- **Comprehensive ablation suite.** Encoder ablation (3 backbones), window size ablation (k=3,5,7), data scaling, and multi-seed stability are all present.\n- **Practical relevance.** The <5ms inference time and no-GPU requirement for structural features have clear deployment value.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **[Major] Gold set results are missing from the submitted paper.** Section 5.7 is a blue-text placeholder: \"Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending. Results will be added upon completion (expected 2026-05-17).\" The conclusion states the gold set \"will determine whether structural features detect real stagnation or merely reconstruct labeling function outputs.\" Without this section, the paper cannot support its title claim. The authors explicitly frame this as the GO/NO-GO gate in their ROADMAP. Submitting with this placeholder is below the threshold for review.\n\n- **[Major] Circular evaluation undermines the headline claim.** The paper's title and abstract assert stagnation signals are \"fundamentally structural,\" but the primary evidence (0.9951 in-domain F1) is acknowledged as circular. Cross-agent transfer (0.967) is offered as non-circular, but this too evaluates against silver labels on the target agent---labels that also suffer from the same LF-feature overlap. The only truly non-circular evaluation is the missing gold set.\n\n- **[Major] The \"circular evaluation\" concept is never formalized.** The paper describes circularity informally (\"structural features overlap with the signals used by labeling functions\") but never defines it precisely. What exactly constitutes \"overlap\"? Is it feature-LF mutual information? Functional dependency? The absence of a formal definition makes the inflation quantification (Table 4 in the task description / not present in the submitted paper) ad hoc. A formal treatment (e.g., defining circularity as the mutual information between feature vector X and LF vote vector Lambda, or showing that structural features can exactly reconstruct LF votes) would strengthen the contribution.\n\n- **[Major] No confidence intervals on main results.** Table 2 (main results) reports point estimates for all methods. Multi-seed stability is mentioned only for structural RF (0.9951 +/- 0.0000) and probe (0.9185 +/- 0.0113) in the text but not in the table. Significance tests between methods are not reported. The paired bootstrap test mentioned in the ROADMAP appears to still be in progress (\"paired-bootstrap cross-domain fix Worker running\"). Without these, the claimed gaps (e.g., structural-probe gap of 8.96pp) cannot be assessed for statistical reliability.\n\n- **[Major] No figures in the submitted paper except pipeline, temporal, and data scaling.** The paper has exactly three figures (pipeline diagram, LF temporal dynamics, data scaling curve). Conspicuously absent: confusion matrices, error analysis visualizations, Sankey diagrams for LF agreement, feature importance bar plots, cross-agent comparison visualizations. For an empirical analysis paper, this visual poverty is a significant weakness. The encoder ablation (Appendix Table 7) has incomplete per-seed detail (\"per-seed detail not recorded\").\n\n- **[Minor] Gold set construction details are insufficient for the submitted version.** The submitted paper mentions a \"650-window gold set with a validated annotation pipeline\" in the limitations section but provides no details about annotator selection, annotation guidelines, adjudication protocol, or sampling strategy. The dream version adds IAA kappa=0.82 and agreement=91%, but these are simulated, not actual.\n\n- **[Minor] Binary stagnation formulation is coarse.** The paper acknowledges this in limitations but does not explore even simple extensions (e.g., a confidence-weighted prediction, a 3-class scheme with an \"ambiguous\" category). Given that conf=1 windows have only 69% gold accuracy (per the dream version), this is a real limitation.\n\n- **[Minor] LLM-as-judge baseline is weak.** Zero-shot Claude Sonnet with no chain-of-thought, no few-shot examples, and a single prompt variant is a strawman. The paper does not explore whether providing the LLM with structural feature values, few-shot examples of stagnation, or explicit rubrics would improve performance. This matters because the 33.3pp gap over LLM-as-judge is a headline number.\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Gold set evaluation (the entire Section 5.7).** This is the single most important missing piece. Without it, the paper cannot distinguish genuine detection from LF reconstruction.\n\n2. **Significance testing between methods.** Paired bootstrap or McNemar's test between structural vs. probe, structural vs. LLM, and probe vs. LLM on the same test set.\n\n3. **Feature importance shift analysis.** The paper_framing mentions cmd_rep_ratio rank shifting from 1->3 on gold vs. LF-derived evaluation, but this analysis is absent from the submitted paper. The appendix says \"Feature importance ranking from RF/GB models is pending a dedicated analysis run.\"\n\n4. **Cross-agent gold evaluation.** Even a small gold set on OpenHands (e.g., 100 windows) would validate the cross-agent transfer claim non-circularly. The dream version acknowledges this is missing and uses \"bounded estimates.\"\n\n5. **LLM-as-judge with chain-of-thought or few-shot.** The current zero-shot baseline is minimal.\n\n6. **Error overlap Venn diagram across all three methods** (structural, probe, LLM). The error analysis in Section 6.3 covers only the probe; the dream version adds a comparison but the submitted paper lacks it.\n\n7. **Calibration analysis.** Are the models' predicted probabilities well-calibrated? This matters for downstream use in intervention systems.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Will the gold set be completed before submission?** The ROADMAP indicates May 16 as the latest start date and May 25 as the deadline. If the gold set is not included, what claim structure survives? The paper currently hedges with \"will determine\" language, which is not acceptable for a submitted paper.\n\n2. **How do you define \"circular evaluation\" formally?** You state structural features \"overlap with\" LF inputs, but 30 features include many that are NOT direct LF inputs (text length statistics, error signals, command type distributions). Can you quantify what fraction of the structural feature variance is explained by the 3 LF votes? A simple experiment: train a model to predict the 3 LF votes from the 30 features and measure R-squared.\n\n3. **Why does the cross-agent transfer constitute non-circular evaluation?** The OpenHands labels are also silver (produced by the same 3 LFs). The LF firing patterns differ, but the labels are still LF-derived. Is the argument that because the LF distributions differ, the classifier cannot trivially reconstruct them? This needs more precision.\n\n4. **What is the stagnation prevalence discrepancy between LF (28.5%) and human annotation?** The dream version reports 33% stagnation in the gold set vs. 28.5% in silver labels. This 4.5pp gap suggests LFs systematically under-label stagnation. Does this affect the inflation quantification?\n\n5. **Why not include a supervised baseline trained on a small gold set?** With 650 gold-annotated windows, a train/test split (e.g., 500/150) could provide a non-circular supervised upper bound.\n\n### Detailed Comments\n\nThe paper occupies an uncomfortable position: it identifies a real and important problem (circular evaluation in weak supervision for agent data), frames it honestly, but then fails to resolve it within the submitted manuscript. The gold set section---which the authors themselves identify as the \"GO/NO-GO gate\"---is a placeholder. This creates a situation where the paper's most important claim (\"stagnation signals are fundamentally structural\") rests entirely on evidence the authors themselves flag as potentially circular.\n\nThe dream/simulated version of the paper is substantially stronger, showing a 0.91 gold F1 for structural GB, a clear hierarchy preservation, and an 8.5pp circularity quantification. If these results materialize, the paper would be in the weak accept range. But the submitted version does not contain them.\n\nThe experimental methodology is otherwise sound. The weak supervision pipeline is well-designed, the three LFs target complementary signals, and the systematic comparison across structural/probe/LLM is thorough. The temporal dynamics finding is genuinely novel and the negative result on temporal label models is well-analyzed. The cmd_rep ablation on OpenHands is one of the strongest experimental contributions.\n\nHowever, several design choices weaken the conclusions. The LLM-as-judge baseline is minimal (zero-shot, single prompt, no CoT), making the 33.3pp gap less impressive than it appears. The binary classification formulation discards useful gradient information. The cross-agent evaluation uses only 2 agents from the same domain (software engineering on SWE-bench), limiting generalization claims.\n\nThe paper's framing as an \"empirical analysis paper\" is appropriate given the pivot from temporal label models (which failed) to structural signal discovery. But empirical analysis papers live or die on the quality and completeness of their empirical evidence. A paper that acknowledges its primary evaluation is circular and then does not include the non-circular evaluation is, by its own standard, incomplete.\n\nRegarding reproducibility: the submitted paper provides window size k=5, stride 1, GRU hyperparameters (hidden=256, dropout=0.1, lr=1e-3, batch=32, patience=5), and the full 30-feature list in the appendix. The LLM-as-judge prompt is not provided. Train/test split details are incomplete (the split strategy---random, by instance, by trajectory---is not specified). The labeling function thresholds for cmd_rep and file_diff are not given.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Preliminary Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe score of 3 (Weak Reject) reflects the fundamental incompleteness of the empirical evaluation. The gold set---which the paper itself positions as essential---is missing. If the gold results from the dream simulation materialize and match the simulated numbers, the paper would merit reconsideration at 4/6. The confidence is 4/5 because the missing gold evaluation is a clear, verifiable gap, and the circularity concern is well-articulated by the authors themselves. The remaining uncertainty is whether the gold results, once available, will validate or overturn the claimed hierarchy."}],"totalDurationMs":130937,"totalTokens":70255,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1478,"cache_read_input_tokens":65848,"output_tokens":2928,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1478},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2928,"cache_read_input_tokens":65848,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1478,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1478,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
