{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N7EgbKWRQihTMUsrWaVab2","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed for a thorough review. Let me compile my assessment.\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper studies progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using weak supervision. The central empirical finding is that 30 hand-crafted structural features achieve near-perfect stagnation detection (0.995 in-domain, 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1), decisively outperforming neural embedding probes and LLM-as-judge baselines. The paper also characterizes temporal dynamics of labeling functions and reports a negative result on temporal label models. The work is well-motivated and the circular evaluation problem is honestly discussed, but the paper is submitted without its planned gold-standard human evaluation, which fundamentally undermines the central claims.\n\n### Strengths\n- **Honest treatment of circularity**: The paper is commendably transparent about the circular evaluation problem. The authors explicitly acknowledge that the 0.9951 structural F1 reflects \"LF-reconstructibility, not ground-truth detection accuracy\" and mark structural results with daggers throughout. This level of methodological honesty is rare and appreciated.\n- **Well-structured related work**: Section 2 is substantially improved from what the task summary suggested. It covers four subsections (Agent Monitoring, PRMs, Data Programming, Anomaly Detection) with 30+ references, clearly positioning the work against the PRM literature and sequential weak supervision extensions. The differentiation from PRMs (window-level vs. step-level, no outcome labels, monitoring vs. training signal) is convincing.\n- **Cross-agent transfer as partial validation**: The SWE-Agent-to-OpenHands transfer experiment (0.967 macro-F1) provides a meaningful non-circular evaluation axis, since LF firing patterns differ substantially between agents.\n- **Informative negative result on temporal label models**: The TLM failure analysis is thorough---three implementations tested, root cause identified (low inter-LF conflict in 3-LF regime), and clear constraints stated for future work. This is a genuinely useful negative finding.\n- **cmd_rep ablation is insightful**: The discovery that a labeling function validated on one agent becomes adversarial on another (+17.6pp improvement upon removal on OpenHands) is a practical and actionable finding for weak supervision practitioners.\n- **Clean writing and logical flow**: The paper is generally well-written, with clear section structure, consistent notation, and appropriate hedging of claims. The progression from problem to method to results to analysis is easy to follow.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **(Major) Missing gold set evaluation -- the paper's own identified critical path is unmet.** Section 5.8 contains a blue placeholder: \"Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending.\" The paper's own framing, limitations, and conclusion all emphasize that gold evaluation is needed to determine whether structural features detect \"real stagnation or merely reconstruct labeling function outputs.\" Without this, the paper's central claim---that stagnation signals are \"fundamentally structural\"---rests entirely on silver labels that the authors themselves argue are circular. The cross-agent transfer partially addresses this, but the paper explicitly identifies the gold set as the \"definitive non-circular evaluation.\" Submitting without it is a critical gap. The \"dream\" version of this paper includes gold results (kappa=0.82, structural F1=0.91 on gold), demonstrating the authors know this data is essential.\n\n- **(Major) The primary finding may be tautological without gold validation.** The headline result---structural features at 0.995 F1---is acknowledged to be circular. The paper argues cross-agent transfer breaks circularity, but this is only partially true: the OpenHands labels are also LF-derived, just with different firing distributions. The structural features could be learning to reconstruct LF logic in both domains rather than detecting genuine stagnation. Without human ground truth, we cannot distinguish \"structural features detect stagnation\" from \"structural features reconstruct the heuristics that define our labels.\"\n\n- **(Major) Narrow task domain limits generalizability claims.** Both agents operate on SWE-bench software engineering tasks. The paper's broader framing---\"redirects agent monitoring from costly representation learning toward lightweight structural approaches\"---is not supported by the evidence. In domains without clear structural markers (web browsing, data analysis, multi-agent collaboration), the structural advantage may vanish. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but does not sufficiently temper the abstract and conclusion claims.\n\n- **(Minor) Only 3 labeling functions, and one is privileged.** Outcome backpropagation uses task outcome, which is unavailable at inference time. The paper discusses this (Section 6.4), but the 3-LF setup creates a constrained regime where (a) the TLM negative result may be an artifact of too few LFs rather than a fundamental insight, and (b) the weak supervision framework is closer to a hand-engineered heuristic system than a principled data programming pipeline. With only 3 binary LFs and 95.1% agreement, the \"weak supervision\" framing overstates the sophistication of what is essentially a rule-based labeling scheme with majority vote.\n\n- **(Minor) Figures are adequate but sparse for a top-venue empirical paper.** The paper includes 3 figures (pipeline overview, LF temporal dynamics, data scaling curve). For an 8-page empirical study, this is thin. Missing visualizations that would strengthen the paper: a confusion matrix heatmap for silver-vs-gold labels, a dumbbell/waterfall plot showing the inflation gap, example trajectory windows illustrating stagnation vs. progress, and cross-agent feature importance comparison. The pipeline figure is serviceable but not publication-quality for a top venue.\n\n- **(Minor) The LLM-as-judge baseline is weak and potentially unfair.** A single zero-shot Claude Sonnet baseline at 0.663 F1 is used to claim a 33.3pp advantage for structural features. No few-shot prompting, no chain-of-thought, no prompt optimization. The LLM baseline is a strawman that inflates the structural advantage. A fairer comparison would include few-shot examples or structured prompts.\n\n- **(Minor) Inconsistency in encoder naming.** The abstract and method section refer to \"Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B,\" but the appendix Table 6 uses \"SFR-Embedding (0.6B).\" These appear to be the same model but the inconsistent naming is confusing and suggests rushed preparation.\n\n### Novelty Assessment\nThe core insight---that circular evaluation inflates weak supervision metrics when features overlap with LF inputs---is not new in principle. Label leakage, train-test contamination, and Goodhart's law are well-established concepts. What the paper contributes is a *quantification* of this phenomenon in a specific applied setting (agent trajectory monitoring). The 0.995-to-??? gap (unknown without gold) would be a compelling empirical contribution *if the gold set results were present.*\n\nThe finding that structural features suffice for stagnation detection is interesting but not deeply surprising: if stagnation is defined by structural heuristics (command repetition, file diffs), then structural features will detect it. The novelty depends critically on whether the gold set confirms that these structural signals correspond to *human-perceived* stagnation, not just LF-defined stagnation. Without gold results, the paper's novelty reduces to \"we built a rule-based labeling system and showed that features correlated with those rules predict those labels well,\" which is circular and low-novelty.\n\nThe LF temporal dynamics finding (leading/lagging/concurrent roles, agent-specific reversals) is genuinely novel and interesting. The cmd_rep role reversal on OpenHands is a practical insight for weak supervision practitioners. However, as a standalone contribution, it is incremental.\n\nThe TLM negative result has value as a well-documented negative finding, but its scope is limited: 3 LFs in a low-conflict regime may simply be too constrained for temporal modeling to help, making this more of an engineering boundary condition than a fundamental insight about temporal label models.\n\nOverall novelty: moderate. The framing is well-done, but the contribution is primarily empirical, and the key empirical validation (gold set) is missing.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\nThe related work section is actually quite thorough (contrary to the task summary's description of \"very brief ~3 short paragraphs\"---the actual paper has 4 substantive subsections). Specific gaps:\n\n1. **Goodhart's law / metric gaming literature**: The circular evaluation phenomenon is closely related to Goodhart's law (\"when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\"). No connection is drawn to this well-known concept, which would strengthen the framing.\n\n2. **Label leakage in ML evaluation**: The train-test contamination / data leakage literature (e.g., Kaufman et al. 2012, \"Leakage in Data Mining\") is directly relevant but not cited. The paper would benefit from positioning circular evaluation as a specific instance of a known evaluation pitfall.\n\n3. **Active learning and annotation efficiency**: The paper builds a 650-window gold set but does not cite the active learning literature on efficient human annotation, which could inform the annotation strategy.\n\n4. **Missing non-SE agent monitoring work**: AgentBench (Liu et al. 2023), OSWorld (Xie et al. 2024), and other multi-domain agent benchmarks are relevant for discussing generalizability limitations.\n\n5. **Snorkel MeTaL / Snorkel variants**: The paper cites the original Snorkel but not later extensions like MeTaL (multi-task weak supervision) that handle more complex label structures.\n\nNo broken citations (\"??\") were found in the current LaTeX source, which contradicts the task summary. The references.bib contains all 35 cited works with proper entries.\n\n### Presentation Issues\n1. **Blue placeholder text in Section 5.8**: The `\\goldset{...}` command renders a blue bracketed placeholder that says \"Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending.\" This is clearly a draft artifact and must not appear in a submitted paper. If the gold results are not ready, the section should be removed or replaced with a concrete plan.\n\n2. **Encoder naming inconsistency**: \"Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\" in the main text vs. \"SFR-Embedding (0.6B)\" in the appendix. This must be resolved.\n\n3. **The data scaling figure (Figure 3) shows the structural RF ceiling as a dashed line at ~0.995 without the circular evaluation caveat annotation.** This visually implies the structural ceiling is a meaningful target, when the paper's own analysis says it is an artifact. The figure should annotate this.\n\n4. **Table 2 (main results) uses \"---\" for many cross-agent cells** without explanation of why those experiments were not run. Mean-pool, LLM-judge, and cosine similarity have no cross-agent numbers, weakening the comparison.\n\n5. **The paper title changed between main.tex and the dream version.** The current title (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study...\") is better than the dream version but at 17 words is quite long.\n\n6. **Section 6 (Analysis) duplicates information from Section 5.** The circular evaluation discussion in Section 6.1 largely repeats the caveat in Section 5's opening paragraph and the discussion in Section 5.2. This could be tightened.\n\n7. **The LF temporal dynamics figure (Figure 2) is well-designed** with the role reversal arrow clearly shown. This is the strongest visual element in the paper.\n\n8. **Appendix Table 5 (feature list)** notes \"Feature importance ranking from RF/GB models is pending a dedicated analysis run.\" Incomplete appendix tables suggest rushed preparation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. **When will the gold set results be available, and what is your contingency if they contradict the structural-dominance finding?** The paper's own framing identifies this as a GO/NO-GO gate. If structural features drop to, say, 0.75 on gold (similar to the probe), would you reframe the paper?\n\n2. **Why was the LLM-as-judge baseline limited to zero-shot?** A few-shot baseline with 5-10 examples would be a much fairer comparison. Did you attempt this?\n\n3. **The cross-agent \"non-circular\" evaluation still uses LF-derived labels on OpenHands.** Can you clarify why this is non-circular? The structural features could be learning generalizable LF-reconstruction logic rather than generalizable stagnation detection.\n\n4. **What is the overlap between structural features and LF inputs, exactly?** You state \"several structural features overlap,\" but how many of the 30 features are direct inputs to the 3 LFs vs. independent signals? A quantitative decomposition would strengthen the circularity analysis.\n\n5. **The 28.5% vs. 6.6% stagnation rate difference between agents is striking.** Is this a genuine behavioral difference, or could it reflect LF calibration issues? If the same human annotators labeled both agents, would the gap persist?\n\n### Detailed Comments\nThis paper tackles a real and practical problem---detecting when LLM agents are stuck---with a methodologically sound approach. The authors deserve credit for identifying and honestly discussing the circular evaluation issue, which many papers in weak supervision sweep under the rug. The related work coverage is thorough and the positioning against the PRM literature is well-argued.\n\nHowever, the paper as submitted has a fundamental structural problem: it identifies gold-standard human evaluation as its critical validation, builds the infrastructure for it, and then submits without it. The paper reads as 85% complete. Every major claim is hedged with \"pending gold set validation,\" the conclusion lists it as the most critical future direction, and Section 5.8 contains a literal placeholder. This is not a minor omission---it is the experiment that determines whether the paper's central finding is real or an artifact.\n\nThe \"structural signals suffice\" finding, while framed as surprising, has a simpler explanation the paper does not adequately rule out: the labels are defined by structural heuristics, so structural features predict them well. The cross-agent transfer is the strongest counter-evidence, but it is still evaluated against LF-derived labels. The paper needs the gold set to close this logical gap.\n\nThe temporal dynamics analysis (Finding 2) and the TLM negative result (Finding 3) are solid secondary contributions but cannot carry the paper alone. They are interesting observations within a specific weak supervision setup rather than broadly generalizable insights.\n\nFor a top venue at 25% acceptance, the paper would need either (a) the gold set results confirming the structural hierarchy, or (b) a fundamentally different framing that does not rely on gold validation (e.g., positioning as a methodological contribution about circular evaluation in weak supervision, with the agent monitoring as a case study). The current framing promises an answer the paper does not deliver.\n\nThe writing quality is generally high, with clear explanations and appropriate statistical rigor (Kruskal-Wallis tests, multi-seed stability, Bonferroni corrections). The three figures that exist are adequate, though a top-venue empirical paper would benefit from 5-6 figures. The pipeline figure effectively communicates the system design.\n\nIf the authors can add the gold set results before the deadline and they confirm the structural advantage (even at reduced magnitude, as the dream version suggests with 0.91 on gold), this paper would be a solid contribution worthy of acceptance. Without it, the paper is incomplete.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Preliminary Confidence: 4/5\n```\n\n**Score justification**: 3 (Weak Reject). The paper is well-written, addresses a real problem, and demonstrates unusual methodological honesty about circular evaluation. However, submitting without the gold-standard validation that the paper itself identifies as critical is a fatal gap for a top venue. The central claim (\"stagnation signals are structural\") cannot be distinguished from \"our labels are structural\" without human ground truth. The secondary contributions (temporal dynamics, TLM negative result) are interesting but incremental. If gold results are added and confirm the hierarchy, I would raise to 4 (Weak Accept) or possibly 5.\n\n**Confidence justification**: 4/5. I have carefully read all sections, the appendix, the references, and the project context. I am confident in my assessment of the novelty, related work coverage, and the criticality of the missing gold evaluation. The one source of uncertainty is whether I am undervaluing the cross-agent transfer as a circularity-breaking mechanism---the authors may have a stronger argument for this than the paper currently presents."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac2b26c3beab028da (use SendMessage with to: 'ac2b26c3beab028da' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78251\ntool_uses: 30\nduration_ms: 179562</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa0581-5224-4e41-930b-491fc2855128","uuid":"b47deacc-fc72-49d2-924a-a18e4cca29ba","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:33:08.366Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty**, **Related Work**, and **Presentation/Writing**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Sequential Agent Data\"\n8 pages, anonymous, dated May 14 2026.\n\nThe paper studies progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using weak supervision (labeling functions). The core finding: standard LF-derived evaluation massively overstates detector performance due to \"circular evaluation\"—training and evaluating on labels derived from features that overlap with classifier inputs. They construct a 650-window human-annotated gold set and show:\n\n1. **Inflation**: Structural classifiers drop from F1=0.995 to 0.74-0.76 on gold (25.5pp inflation); probes drop 15.5pp; LLM-as-judge *improves* by 6.8pp on gold.\n2. **Hierarchy collapse**: Structural-over-probe gap reverses from +9pp to -1pp; structural-over-LLM collapses from +24.3pp to +3pp. All methods converge to 0.73-0.77 band.\n3. **Temporal dynamics validated**: LF temporal roles (cmd_rep leading, outcome_bp lagging) preserved on gold (KW p=0.003).\n4. **Temporal label models fail**: TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM all fail to beat majority vote.\n\nContributions claimed: (1) first quantification of circular eval inflation in weak supervision for sequential agent data (mean 21.5pp); (2) 650-window gold benchmark with IAA κ=0.72; (3) temporal dynamics are genuine but agent-specific; (4) negative result on temporal label models.\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages)\n1. Introduction (p1-2): Motivates circular evaluation problem, lists 4 findings\n2. Related Work (p2): Three short paragraphs — Agent Monitoring, Weak Supervision/Data Programming, LLM-as-Judge\n3. Task and Data (p2-3): Problem formulation, 3 LFs described, agent trajectories (SWE-Agent, OpenHands), gold annotation (Table 1)\n4. Methods (p3): Four method families described briefly\n5. Experiments (p3-7): Sections 5.1-5.11 covering all major results\n6. Discussion (p7): Three discussion points\n7. Limitations (p7): One paragraph\n8. Conclusion (p8): One paragraph\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: Gold set stats\n- Table 2: LF-Human confusion matrix\n- Table 3: Per-LF agreement\n- Table 4: Inflation/deflation summary (central result)\n- Table 5: Pairwise gap collapse\n- Table 6: Structural classifiers on gold (per-class)\n- Table 7: Probe performance across encoders\n- Table 8: Cross-agent evaluation\n- Table 9: F1_stag breakdown\n- Table 10: Error distribution\n- Table 11: Temporal dynamics\n\n**NOTE: The paper has NO figures at all — only tables.** The experiment plan described many figures (Sankey diagram, heatmaps, scatter plots, Venn diagram, dumbbell plot, radar chart, waterfall chart, etc.) but none appear in the paper.\n\n## Related Work Coverage in Paper\nThe paper's Related Work section (Section 2) is very brief (~3 short paragraphs):\n- **Agent Monitoring**: Mentions SlopCodeBench, CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD, AgentPRM, Reflexion, LATS\n- **Weak Supervision**: Mentions Snorkel paradigm, conditional independence assumption\n- **LLM-as-Judge**: One sentence noting general trend + their baseline\n\n## Key Claims to Assess for Novelty\n1. \"First quantification of circular evaluation inflation in weak supervision for sequential data\" — Is this truly novel? Circular evaluation / label leakage is a known concern in ML.\n2. \"First human-annotated benchmark for agent stagnation\" — Novel resource contribution\n3. \"LF temporal dynamics are genuine but agent-specific\" — How novel vs incremental?\n4. \"Negative result on temporal label models\" — Value of negative results\n\n## Missing References to Check\n- The paper has broken citations: \"??\" appears in the introduction (page 1)\n- No reference list is included in the 8-page paper\n- Snorkel (Ratner et al.) is mentioned but not cited with a number\n- SlopCodeBench, CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD, AgentPRM, Reflexion, LATS are mentioned by name but without proper citations\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n### 1. Novelty & Contribution\n- Is \"circular evaluation in weak supervision for sequential data\" a genuinely new insight?\n- How does this relate to known problems: label leakage, train-test contamination, Goodhart's law in evaluation?\n- Is the contribution primarily empirical (showing something happens) or methodological (solving it)?\n- Is the gold benchmark a meaningful contribution?\n- Is the negative result (TLMs fail) valuable?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are key relevant works cited? Missing citations?\n- Is positioning against data programming literature adequate?\n- Are the broken citations (\"??\") a serious issue?\n- Is the comparison to existing agent monitoring work sufficient?\n\n### 3. Presentation & Writing Quality\n- Paper structure and flow\n- The complete absence of figures — how severe is this?\n- Clarity of the \"circular evaluation\" explanation\n- Are claims appropriately hedged?\n- Is the paper appropriately scoped for a top venue?\n\n### 4. Significance & Impact\n- Who would benefit from these findings?\n- Would this change practice in the field?\n- Is this a cautionary tale or does it advance the state of the art?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 4-8 items, each with severity: Major/Minor]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph on novelty]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[What's missing from related work]\n\n### Presentation Issues\n[Specific issues with writing/figures/structure]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Paragraph-form deeper analysis]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Preliminary Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"ac2b26c3beab028da","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed for a thorough review. Let me compile my assessment.\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper studies progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using weak supervision. The central empirical finding is that 30 hand-crafted structural features achieve near-perfect stagnation detection (0.995 in-domain, 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1), decisively outperforming neural embedding probes and LLM-as-judge baselines. The paper also characterizes temporal dynamics of labeling functions and reports a negative result on temporal label models. The work is well-motivated and the circular evaluation problem is honestly discussed, but the paper is submitted without its planned gold-standard human evaluation, which fundamentally undermines the central claims.\n\n### Strengths\n- **Honest treatment of circularity**: The paper is commendably transparent about the circular evaluation problem. The authors explicitly acknowledge that the 0.9951 structural F1 reflects \"LF-reconstructibility, not ground-truth detection accuracy\" and mark structural results with daggers throughout. This level of methodological honesty is rare and appreciated.\n- **Well-structured related work**: Section 2 is substantially improved from what the task summary suggested. It covers four subsections (Agent Monitoring, PRMs, Data Programming, Anomaly Detection) with 30+ references, clearly positioning the work against the PRM literature and sequential weak supervision extensions. The differentiation from PRMs (window-level vs. step-level, no outcome labels, monitoring vs. training signal) is convincing.\n- **Cross-agent transfer as partial validation**: The SWE-Agent-to-OpenHands transfer experiment (0.967 macro-F1) provides a meaningful non-circular evaluation axis, since LF firing patterns differ substantially between agents.\n- **Informative negative result on temporal label models**: The TLM failure analysis is thorough---three implementations tested, root cause identified (low inter-LF conflict in 3-LF regime), and clear constraints stated for future work. This is a genuinely useful negative finding.\n- **cmd_rep ablation is insightful**: The discovery that a labeling function validated on one agent becomes adversarial on another (+17.6pp improvement upon removal on OpenHands) is a practical and actionable finding for weak supervision practitioners.\n- **Clean writing and logical flow**: The paper is generally well-written, with clear section structure, consistent notation, and appropriate hedging of claims. The progression from problem to method to results to analysis is easy to follow.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **(Major) Missing gold set evaluation -- the paper's own identified critical path is unmet.** Section 5.8 contains a blue placeholder: \"Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending.\" The paper's own framing, limitations, and conclusion all emphasize that gold evaluation is needed to determine whether structural features detect \"real stagnation or merely reconstruct labeling function outputs.\" Without this, the paper's central claim---that stagnation signals are \"fundamentally structural\"---rests entirely on silver labels that the authors themselves argue are circular. The cross-agent transfer partially addresses this, but the paper explicitly identifies the gold set as the \"definitive non-circular evaluation.\" Submitting without it is a critical gap. The \"dream\" version of this paper includes gold results (kappa=0.82, structural F1=0.91 on gold), demonstrating the authors know this data is essential.\n\n- **(Major) The primary finding may be tautological without gold validation.** The headline result---structural features at 0.995 F1---is acknowledged to be circular. The paper argues cross-agent transfer breaks circularity, but this is only partially true: the OpenHands labels are also LF-derived, just with different firing distributions. The structural features could be learning to reconstruct LF logic in both domains rather than detecting genuine stagnation. Without human ground truth, we cannot distinguish \"structural features detect stagnation\" from \"structural features reconstruct the heuristics that define our labels.\"\n\n- **(Major) Narrow task domain limits generalizability claims.** Both agents operate on SWE-bench software engineering tasks. The paper's broader framing---\"redirects agent monitoring from costly representation learning toward lightweight structural approaches\"---is not supported by the evidence. In domains without clear structural markers (web browsing, data analysis, multi-agent collaboration), the structural advantage may vanish. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but does not sufficiently temper the abstract and conclusion claims.\n\n- **(Minor) Only 3 labeling functions, and one is privileged.** Outcome backpropagation uses task outcome, which is unavailable at inference time. The paper discusses this (Section 6.4), but the 3-LF setup creates a constrained regime where (a) the TLM negative result may be an artifact of too few LFs rather than a fundamental insight, and (b) the weak supervision framework is closer to a hand-engineered heuristic system than a principled data programming pipeline. With only 3 binary LFs and 95.1% agreement, the \"weak supervision\" framing overstates the sophistication of what is essentially a rule-based labeling scheme with majority vote.\n\n- **(Minor) Figures are adequate but sparse for a top-venue empirical paper.** The paper includes 3 figures (pipeline overview, LF temporal dynamics, data scaling curve). For an 8-page empirical study, this is thin. Missing visualizations that would strengthen the paper: a confusion matrix heatmap for silver-vs-gold labels, a dumbbell/waterfall plot showing the inflation gap, example trajectory windows illustrating stagnation vs. progress, and cross-agent feature importance comparison. The pipeline figure is serviceable but not publication-quality for a top venue.\n\n- **(Minor) The LLM-as-judge baseline is weak and potentially unfair.** A single zero-shot Claude Sonnet baseline at 0.663 F1 is used to claim a 33.3pp advantage for structural features. No few-shot prompting, no chain-of-thought, no prompt optimization. The LLM baseline is a strawman that inflates the structural advantage. A fairer comparison would include few-shot examples or structured prompts.\n\n- **(Minor) Inconsistency in encoder naming.** The abstract and method section refer to \"Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B,\" but the appendix Table 6 uses \"SFR-Embedding (0.6B).\" These appear to be the same model but the inconsistent naming is confusing and suggests rushed preparation.\n\n### Novelty Assessment\nThe core insight---that circular evaluation inflates weak supervision metrics when features overlap with LF inputs---is not new in principle. Label leakage, train-test contamination, and Goodhart's law are well-established concepts. What the paper contributes is a *quantification* of this phenomenon in a specific applied setting (agent trajectory monitoring). The 0.995-to-??? gap (unknown without gold) would be a compelling empirical contribution *if the gold set results were present.*\n\nThe finding that structural features suffice for stagnation detection is interesting but not deeply surprising: if stagnation is defined by structural heuristics (command repetition, file diffs), then structural features will detect it. The novelty depends critically on whether the gold set confirms that these structural signals correspond to *human-perceived* stagnation, not just LF-defined stagnation. Without gold results, the paper's novelty reduces to \"we built a rule-based labeling system and showed that features correlated with those rules predict those labels well,\" which is circular and low-novelty.\n\nThe LF temporal dynamics finding (leading/lagging/concurrent roles, agent-specific reversals) is genuinely novel and interesting. The cmd_rep role reversal on OpenHands is a practical insight for weak supervision practitioners. However, as a standalone contribution, it is incremental.\n\nThe TLM negative result has value as a well-documented negative finding, but its scope is limited: 3 LFs in a low-conflict regime may simply be too constrained for temporal modeling to help, making this more of an engineering boundary condition than a fundamental insight about temporal label models.\n\nOverall novelty: moderate. The framing is well-done, but the contribution is primarily empirical, and the key empirical validation (gold set) is missing.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\nThe related work section is actually quite thorough (contrary to the task summary's description of \"very brief ~3 short paragraphs\"---the actual paper has 4 substantive subsections). Specific gaps:\n\n1. **Goodhart's law / metric gaming literature**: The circular evaluation phenomenon is closely related to Goodhart's law (\"when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\"). No connection is drawn to this well-known concept, which would strengthen the framing.\n\n2. **Label leakage in ML evaluation**: The train-test contamination / data leakage literature (e.g., Kaufman et al. 2012, \"Leakage in Data Mining\") is directly relevant but not cited. The paper would benefit from positioning circular evaluation as a specific instance of a known evaluation pitfall.\n\n3. **Active learning and annotation efficiency**: The paper builds a 650-window gold set but does not cite the active learning literature on efficient human annotation, which could inform the annotation strategy.\n\n4. **Missing non-SE agent monitoring work**: AgentBench (Liu et al. 2023), OSWorld (Xie et al. 2024), and other multi-domain agent benchmarks are relevant for discussing generalizability limitations.\n\n5. **Snorkel MeTaL / Snorkel variants**: The paper cites the original Snorkel but not later extensions like MeTaL (multi-task weak supervision) that handle more complex label structures.\n\nNo broken citations (\"??\") were found in the current LaTeX source, which contradicts the task summary. The references.bib contains all 35 cited works with proper entries.\n\n### Presentation Issues\n1. **Blue placeholder text in Section 5.8**: The `\\goldset{...}` command renders a blue bracketed placeholder that says \"Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending.\" This is clearly a draft artifact and must not appear in a submitted paper. If the gold results are not ready, the section should be removed or replaced with a concrete plan.\n\n2. **Encoder naming inconsistency**: \"Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\" in the main text vs. \"SFR-Embedding (0.6B)\" in the appendix. This must be resolved.\n\n3. **The data scaling figure (Figure 3) shows the structural RF ceiling as a dashed line at ~0.995 without the circular evaluation caveat annotation.** This visually implies the structural ceiling is a meaningful target, when the paper's own analysis says it is an artifact. The figure should annotate this.\n\n4. **Table 2 (main results) uses \"---\" for many cross-agent cells** without explanation of why those experiments were not run. Mean-pool, LLM-judge, and cosine similarity have no cross-agent numbers, weakening the comparison.\n\n5. **The paper title changed between main.tex and the dream version.** The current title (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study...\") is better than the dream version but at 17 words is quite long.\n\n6. **Section 6 (Analysis) duplicates information from Section 5.** The circular evaluation discussion in Section 6.1 largely repeats the caveat in Section 5's opening paragraph and the discussion in Section 5.2. This could be tightened.\n\n7. **The LF temporal dynamics figure (Figure 2) is well-designed** with the role reversal arrow clearly shown. This is the strongest visual element in the paper.\n\n8. **Appendix Table 5 (feature list)** notes \"Feature importance ranking from RF/GB models is pending a dedicated analysis run.\" Incomplete appendix tables suggest rushed preparation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. **When will the gold set results be available, and what is your contingency if they contradict the structural-dominance finding?** The paper's own framing identifies this as a GO/NO-GO gate. If structural features drop to, say, 0.75 on gold (similar to the probe), would you reframe the paper?\n\n2. **Why was the LLM-as-judge baseline limited to zero-shot?** A few-shot baseline with 5-10 examples would be a much fairer comparison. Did you attempt this?\n\n3. **The cross-agent \"non-circular\" evaluation still uses LF-derived labels on OpenHands.** Can you clarify why this is non-circular? The structural features could be learning generalizable LF-reconstruction logic rather than generalizable stagnation detection.\n\n4. **What is the overlap between structural features and LF inputs, exactly?** You state \"several structural features overlap,\" but how many of the 30 features are direct inputs to the 3 LFs vs. independent signals? A quantitative decomposition would strengthen the circularity analysis.\n\n5. **The 28.5% vs. 6.6% stagnation rate difference between agents is striking.** Is this a genuine behavioral difference, or could it reflect LF calibration issues? If the same human annotators labeled both agents, would the gap persist?\n\n### Detailed Comments\nThis paper tackles a real and practical problem---detecting when LLM agents are stuck---with a methodologically sound approach. The authors deserve credit for identifying and honestly discussing the circular evaluation issue, which many papers in weak supervision sweep under the rug. The related work coverage is thorough and the positioning against the PRM literature is well-argued.\n\nHowever, the paper as submitted has a fundamental structural problem: it identifies gold-standard human evaluation as its critical validation, builds the infrastructure for it, and then submits without it. The paper reads as 85% complete. Every major claim is hedged with \"pending gold set validation,\" the conclusion lists it as the most critical future direction, and Section 5.8 contains a literal placeholder. This is not a minor omission---it is the experiment that determines whether the paper's central finding is real or an artifact.\n\nThe \"structural signals suffice\" finding, while framed as surprising, has a simpler explanation the paper does not adequately rule out: the labels are defined by structural heuristics, so structural features predict them well. The cross-agent transfer is the strongest counter-evidence, but it is still evaluated against LF-derived labels. The paper needs the gold set to close this logical gap.\n\nThe temporal dynamics analysis (Finding 2) and the TLM negative result (Finding 3) are solid secondary contributions but cannot carry the paper alone. They are interesting observations within a specific weak supervision setup rather than broadly generalizable insights.\n\nFor a top venue at 25% acceptance, the paper would need either (a) the gold set results confirming the structural hierarchy, or (b) a fundamentally different framing that does not rely on gold validation (e.g., positioning as a methodological contribution about circular evaluation in weak supervision, with the agent monitoring as a case study). The current framing promises an answer the paper does not deliver.\n\nThe writing quality is generally high, with clear explanations and appropriate statistical rigor (Kruskal-Wallis tests, multi-seed stability, Bonferroni corrections). The three figures that exist are adequate, though a top-venue empirical paper would benefit from 5-6 figures. The pipeline figure effectively communicates the system design.\n\nIf the authors can add the gold set results before the deadline and they confirm the structural advantage (even at reduced magnitude, as the dream version suggests with 0.91 on gold), this paper would be a solid contribution worthy of acceptance. Without it, the paper is incomplete.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Preliminary Confidence: 4/5\n```\n\n**Score justification**: 3 (Weak Reject). The paper is well-written, addresses a real problem, and demonstrates unusual methodological honesty about circular evaluation. However, submitting without the gold-standard validation that the paper itself identifies as critical is a fatal gap for a top venue. The central claim (\"stagnation signals are structural\") cannot be distinguished from \"our labels are structural\" without human ground truth. The secondary contributions (temporal dynamics, TLM negative result) are interesting but incremental. If gold results are added and confirm the hierarchy, I would raise to 4 (Weak Accept) or possibly 5.\n\n**Confidence justification**: 4/5. I have carefully read all sections, the appendix, the references, and the project context. I am confident in my assessment of the novelty, related work coverage, and the criticality of the missing gold evaluation. The one source of uncertainty is whether I am undervaluing the cross-agent transfer as a circularity-breaking mechanism---the authors may have a stronger argument for this than the paper currently presents."}],"totalDurationMs":179562,"totalTokens":78251,"totalToolUseCount":30,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2277,"cache_read_input_tokens":72128,"output_tokens":3843,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2277},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3843,"cache_read_input_tokens":72128,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2277,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2277,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":16,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":2,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014suDqCHRQkQA3VGFqT5ekz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have returned their independent reviews. However, I notice something important: both R1 and R2 appear to have confused the paper they're reviewing. They seem to have accessed a different version of the paper from the file system (possibly an earlier draft) that still has the gold set as \"pending.\" But the PDF I was given to review is the FINAL version that ALREADY CONTAINS the gold set results. The paper I read clearly has:\n\n- Table 1: Gold set annotation statistics (N=650, κ=0.72)\n- Table 2: LF-Human confusion matrix\n- Tables 4-11 with all gold evaluation results\n\n\n- All sections 5.1-5.11 complete\n\nThe reviewers went exploring in the project directory and found an earlier version of the paper where the gold set was still pending. I need to reconcile their reviews with the actual paper content I was provided, since they've been critiquing an outdated draft rather than the final submission.\n\nLooking at what's actually in the PDF I reviewed: all gold results are complete, there are 11 tables with full data, no figures at all, 8 pages total, some broken citations with \"??\" on page 1, and no visible reference list. The reviewers' comments about missing gold sets and incomplete sections don't apply to this version.\n\nNow I'm moving into the roundtable process. I've got the independent reviews done, though they need correction for the content mismatch. Next I'll cross-share the findings between reviewers, identify where they agree or disagree, and synthesize their observations while accounting for the fact that they were reviewing an outdated source file rather than the actual submitted PDF. Now I'm separating what's actually wrong with the paper from what was based on misreading an older version. The circular evaluation concept needs formal mathematical definition, the results lack confidence intervals and significance tests, and there are no visualizations at all. Several of R1's critiques about missing gold sets and broken citations were based on outdated source files, but the valid methodological gaps remain—no feature importance table despite being mentioned in text, minimal LLM baseline with only zero-shot prompting, and missing details on the LLM prompt itself.\n\nFor R2's concerns, the novelty issue is real: circular evaluation and label leakage aren't new concepts, so the contribution is really just quantifying them in this specific domain. The related work genuinely misses connections to Goodhart's law and active learning literature, and the narrow focus on a single domain limits scope.\n\nNow I need to step back and evaluate the paper on its actual merits rather than what the reviewers thought they saw. Let me work through a proper assessment of the methods and experiments as they really are.\n\nThe gold set construction with 650 annotated windows and reasonable inter-rater agreement is solid methodological work that supports the core analysis. The inflation quantification across seven methods shows a consistent 21.5 percentage point gap between labeling function and gold standard performance, and the hierarchy collapse—where methods converge to a narrow 0.73-0.77 band—is a compelling empirical finding. The temporal dynamics analysis held up under scrutiny with statistical significance, and the negative result on TLM is well-explained rather than hand-waved. The cross-agent validation on OpenHands demonstrates the inflation isn't an artifact of the specific benchmark, while the per-class breakdowns and error pattern analysis showing 89 shared errors align with expected annotator disagreement rates, which strengthens the Bayes error interpretation.\n\nOn the flip side, there are some significant gaps: the lack of confidence intervals and significance tests means I'm only reporting point estimates, and claiming 1-3 percentage point differences matter without paired bootstrap or McNemar tests is risky. The circular evaluation concept is described intuitively but never formally defined—there's no mathematical formulation of what circularity is or how to measure feature-LF overlap. The paper is also missing any figures despite being eight pages with eleven tables, which is unusual and makes the key results like the inflation waterfall and gap collapse harder to visualize. There are also broken citations in the introduction and no visible reference list, which suggests formatting issues. Finally, the LLM-as-judge baseline only uses zero-shot Claude Sonnet without exploring other configurations.\n\nThe gold set of 650 windows is on the small side, and while κ=0.72 is substantial agreement, it's not quite \"almost perfect.\" The paper also leaves out some implementation details like window size, train-test split strategy, the exact LLM prompt, and LF thresholds. The binary stagnation formulation is fairly coarse—there's no investigation of graduated or confidence-weighted alternatives. With only three labeling functions, this is admittedly a low-coverage regime, so the findings might shift with more LFs. I'm also noticing the paper doesn't include significance tests between methods on the gold set, doesn't show the feature importance shift table that's mentioned in the text, and lacks a supervised baseline for comparison.\n\nNow looking at the novelty and related work assessment, the paper's honest reframing of the circular evaluation problem is compelling—acknowledging that the primary claim was wrong and explaining why reads as genuine. The 650-window gold benchmark itself is a real contribution for agent stagnation evaluation. That 0.995 to 0.74 drop is striking and effectively communicates the evaluation risk. The analysis covers multiple angles—inflation quantification, hierarchy collapse, temporal dynamics, TLM failure modes, cross-agent patterns, and per-class breakdowns. The whole thing functions as a cautionary tale with solid methodological value. On the flip side, the core insight about how evaluating on derived labels inflates performance is somewhat straightforward once you see it.\n\nThe scope is narrow—just coding agents on SWE-bench, so generalization beyond that domain remains unclear. The paper has serious presentation issues: broken citations, missing references, no figures at all, and thin related work that doesn't connect to Goodhart's law or the broader label leakage literature. The TLM negative result only holds in a specific 3-LF low-conflict regime, limiting what we can conclude. And fundamentally, this is a problem identification paper without a solution—it diagnoses the issue but doesn't fix it.\n\nOn novelty, the specific application to agent trajectories and the quantified 21.5 percentage point inflation are genuinely new, as is the gold benchmark and the temporal dynamics finding. But the core insight—that features correlated with label-generating rules inflate evaluation metrics—is well-established in machine learning. So this is a solid empirical contribution in a new domain rather than a conceptual breakthrough.\n\nFor a top-tier venue with 25% acceptance, the paper has a clear, well-supported central finding and comprehensive results, but the presentation problems and lack of proposed solutions are significant weaknesses that would likely hurt its competitiveness. I'm settling on R1 (Methods) at 3-4/6 since the science is solid but lacks statistical rigor and some methodological details, and R2 (Novelty) at 3-4/6 given the moderate novelty and narrow scope, though it does serve as a valuable cautionary tale. Now I need to synthesize the final roundtable report.\n\nWait, I should double-check something — the review context shows this is a \"worst tier\" scenario where the paper was generated from imagined experiments, and it's been reframed around circular evaluation. Let me verify whether the paper's results actually match what was expected in the YAML file.\n\nChecking the gold set metrics: the random forest F1 of .74, gradient boosting at .76, probe model at .75, and LLM judge at .73 all align with the YAML expectations. The performance inflation for RF is -25.5 points, human agreement sits at 68%, and Cohen's kappa is 0.34 — everything checks out against the specification.\n\nNow verifying that the paper actually covers all the planned experiments: the gold set evaluation spans human agreement assessment, structural model comparisons with RF and GB, probe-based evaluation, LLM-as-judge re-evaluation, cross-agent structural testing, and circular evaluation inflation analysis. All six major experiment categories are present in the paper.\n\nHowever, there are some notable gaps between what was planned and what made it into the final version—the feature importance shift analysis is mentioned in the text but lacks a dedicated table, and many of the visualizations like Sankey diagrams, heatmaps, scatter plots, and other figures that were outlined in the plan don't appear in the paper at all.\n\nNow I'm moving forward to synthesize the reviewer feedback and produce the final report, addressing the misunderstandings from both sides in one comprehensive response.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11062,"cache_read_input_tokens":54055,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11062},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa0581-5224-4e41-930b-491fc2855128","uuid":"eb93b358-c0e0-4022-8d51-25b958cd536e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014suDqCHRQkQA3VGFqT5ekz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers returned their independent assessments. However, I note that both reviewers explored stale source files in the project directory and mistakenly concluded the gold set results were missing. The actual PDF under review already contains all gold set results (Tables 1-11, Sections 5.1-5.11 complete). I'll now run the roundtable synthesis with corrected assessments.\n\nLet me verify experiment coverage against the YAML plan before producing the final report.\n\n**Experiment coverage check (YAML plan vs paper):**\n- LF-Human Agreement: covered (Sec 5.1, Tables 2-3)\n- Structural on Gold: covered (Sec 5.2/5.4, Tables 4, 6)\n- Probe on Gold: covered (Sec 5.5, Table 7)\n- LLM-as-Judge on Gold: covered (Sec 5.6)\n- Cross-Agent on Gold: covered (Sec 5.7, Table 8)\n- Inflation Quantification: covered (Sec 5.2, Table 4)\n- Inter-Annotator Agreement: covered (Sec 3.4, Table 1)\n- Error Pattern Analysis: covered (Sec 5.9, Table 10)\n- F1-Stag Per-Class: covered (Sec 5.8, Table 9)\n- Temporal Dynamics Validation: covered (Sec 5.10, Table 11)\n- Feature Importance Shift: **mentioned in text but no dedicated table** (YAML planned a table)\n- **All planned figures (Sankey, heatmaps, dumbbell, Venn, scatter, radar, waterfall): NONE present**\n\nNow producing the final synthesis report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11062,"cache_read_input_tokens":54055,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11062},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa0581-5224-4e41-930b-491fc2855128","uuid":"f225b920-3997-4514-bc19-cddcd08e493e"}
